My first ascension (Valkyrie; back in 3.1.3, admittedly, when AC -18
obviated the need for reflection and Sokoban didn't exist yet and
air elementals were a pathetic joke) took something of the order of
70k turns, and a large chunk of that was down to my then-inefficient
style for assaulting Gehennom and an equally inefficient approach to
the Planes.  My play was rather incautious (despite being heavily
spoiled).  I was often Burdened (which bumped up the turn count, of
course).  I still genocided sea monsters.  I still quaffed from
fountains willy-nilly.

Even when I play carefully, with much more spoiler-knowledge in my head
and a version of the game with more ways to waste time, my ascensions take
about 50k-70k turns.  Mid-game optimisations (some of which I regard as
necessary; having either Magicbane or Enough Curse Removal is essential,
because otherwise the Rodneyclock can kill you by grinding away all your
curse-removal power, especially if you're Lawful) take up more turns of my
games, and assaulting Gehennom takes up less, than was the case in 3.1.3.
Of course, I could knock that turn count down by things like using wands
of digging instead of my pickaxe when excavating paths through Gehennom.

I'm a *good* Nethack player - I've ascended characters who aren't
Valkyries, although they've all been wombats - but even so, 10k turn
ascensions utterly astound me with their speed, 120k turn ascensions
make me think "What are you *doing* with all those turns?  Did you spend
the entire game Burdened, or something?", and Marvin's success rate gives
me the fear.

I've never seen the Healer quest except in wizard mode.  I've only once
got past the quest with a Wizard that I can recall, and he got waxed by
a minotaur. (But that was in the dim and distant past when spells were
the suck.) 

Despite all this, I still think that pudding farming (which is easily
fixed: don't let clones death-drop, and tag clone-corpses as of minimal
sacrifice value. problem solved. now let's have a hard one, like fixing
the MONSTR values.), extinctionism, and the like are not behaviours
that the game should reward.  If people want Angband (and I *do* want
Angband from time to time, although I play O instead of vanilla), rgra
is ----> over there.

I don't think that the "midgame clock" should ever get as harsh as the
Rodneyclock, but certainly, indefinitely prolonging the mid-game should
to my mind eventually hit a point of at least moderately negative returns
i.e. you start using more resources than you gain.  Marvin and David
would still find the game staggeringly easy.  Newbies would not find it
any harder than they already do.

The game would continue to reward cautiously forthright play as strongly
as it does now. "Hit things and take their stuff; press on, so that you
can get more stuff" would work as well as it does now. "Skulk about
letting your pet kill things until you get your Constitution up" would
work as well as it does now.  "Spend 10k turns on DL10 camped by an
altar" would *appear* to work as well as it does now, but the resources
gained would be balanced by the increased difficulty of the later part
of the mid-game.
